    "file": ".kilo/plans/chatgpt-harness-export.md",
    "patch": "diff --git a/.kilo/plans/chatgpt-harness-export.md b/.kilo/plans/chatgpt-harness-export.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..74a66e8\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/.kilo/plans/chatgpt-harness-export.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@\n+# Export ChatGPT/Claude work-mode habits into coding harnesses\n+\n+## You are not wrong\n+\n+ChatGPT and Claude **in work/code mode** still research, cite, crunch data, then implement. Kilo / OpenCode / similar CLIs **skip to edits**. That is a **harness** difference, not “you used a weaker model.”\n+\n+Coding CLIs are built to mutate a repo. ChatGPT/Claude work mode is built to **observe → analyze → plan → act**, with a Python/data kernel, browsing, and a product prompt that rewards that loop. Same weights in a “just code” loop will look dumber on data and research.\n+\n+ReAct (Yao et al., 2022): **act-only** underperforms **reason+act**. CLI default is closer to act-only.\n+\n+## What transcripts can give you\n+\n+**Portable (habits)**\n+\n+- Read / query data **before** the first edit\n+- Search and cite before inventing facts\n+- Write a short plan with alternatives, then implement\n+- Do not skip CSVs, logs, evals sitting in the task\n+- Long-horizon: research session ≠ patch session, or gated phases in one session\n+\n+**Not portable (product)**\n+\n+- ChatGPT Code Interpreter / Claude analysis runtime\n+- Their search index, memory, canvas, citation UI\n+- Years of RLHF for “helpful analyst”\n+\n+So: you can make Kilo/OpenCode **behave** more like work-mode ChatGPT. You cannot make them **be** ChatGPT by prompt alone.\n+\n+## Recommended path\n+\n+### Phase 1 — Behavior pack (exportable)\n+\n+A small, harness-agnostic pack:\n+\n+1. **Skill:** `observe-before-mutate` — if the task mentions data, transcripts, metrics, or research, forbid `edit` until files are read and a short written analysis exists.\n+2. **Eval gate:** fixture tasks with a CSV + a code request. **Fail** if the first mutating tool is `edit`/`bash` write. **Pass** if read → summarize numbers → then code.\n+3. **Agent split:** `ask`/`plan` for research; `code` only after a written finding. Same idea in OpenCode via their instruction files.\n+\n+Ship as markdown + tests so it copies to Kilo, OpenCode, others.\n+\n+### Phase 2 — Mine *your* transcripts\n+\n+If you drop ChatGPT/Claude exports:\n+\n+- Parse tool order (search / python / read vs patch)\n+- Extract “first 10 actions” histograms\n+- Turn recurring patterns into the skill above (not a giant cloned prompt)\n+\n+This is distillation of **policy**, not weights.\n+\n+### Phase 3 — Data runtime (optional, Kilo-specific)\n+\n+A sandbox Python/notebook MCP so “analyze this table” does not become “rewrite the app.” Without this, the skill can only **force reading**; it cannot **run pandas like ChatGPT**.\n+\n+## What we will not claim\n+\n+- “Kilo becomes as strong as ChatGPT”\n+- Reverse-engineering their tools or scraping live chats\n+- Skipping evals and only adding more AGENTS.md prose\n+\n+## Success\n+\n+On held-out tasks that include data: the agent produces a numeric/qualitative finding **before** the first code change, with sources. Coding-only tasks stay fast.\n+\n+## First implementation (when you leave plan)\n+\n+- Global skill + one eval fixture\n+- Point `code` agent at “observe-before-mutate” when data is in the prompt\n+- No Kilo `dist/` CSS work in this track",
